# Line endings are now a DEPLOYMENT concern, not a taste one: this repo is authored on Windows
# (core.autocrlf=true) and checked out by a Linux CI runner and a Linux host. Without an explicit policy the
# working-tree ending depends on whoever committed the file, and Git's text/binary guess depends on whether a
# NUL byte happens to appear in the first 8000 bytes.
#
# Everything normalizes to LF in the repo and on checkout. Two files are deliberately CRLF because Windows
# tooling reads them directly from the working tree.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ---- source ----
*.cs        text eol=lf
*.csproj    text eol=lf
*.sln       text eol=crlf
*.config    text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.lua       text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# ---- shell ----
# A .sh with CRLF fails on Linux with the famously unhelpful "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M".
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# ---- binary ----
# These are the ones that matter, and game-data/ is where the stakes are: ~1750 .map terrain files plus
# SObj.tbl, authored on Windows and checked out on a Linux host, all read as raw bytes. A single CRLF
# substitution inside one does not fail — it silently corrupts collision for that map, or a lookup table.
# Git's heuristic almost certainly classifies them binary already; "almost certainly" is not a good enough
# guarantee for the thing that decides whether players can walk through walls.
#
# game-data/ carried its own copy of these rules while it was a submodule (attributes are per-repository
# and are NOT inherited across a submodule boundary). It is a plain directory now, so it inherits these
# and the duplicate is gone.
*.map       binary
*.cmp       binary
*.tbl       binary
*.epf       binary
*.pal       binary
*.frm       binary
*.dat       binary
*.db        binary
*.exe       binary
*.dll       binary
*.png       binary
*.gif       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.wav       binary
*.mp3       binary
